;; Writes confined to one run's workdir. Reads left alone.
;;
;; A grant of `Bash(python3:*)` bounds which *command* may run and says nothing
;; about what it may do, because python is a general interpreter: measured on
;; 2026-08-17, a granted `python3` wrote a file outside its workdir in 3 probes
;; of 3. No permission string fixes that — the containment has to be under the
;; interpreter rather than in front of it.
;;
;; Reads stay open on purpose. The session needs the standard library, and the
;; thing being contained is damage to the machine, not curiosity. A profile that
;; also denied reads would break python before it broke anything else.
;;
;; `sandbox-exec` is deprecated by Apple and still the only thing on a stock
;; macOS that does this. If it is ever removed, the fallback is not a narrower
;; permission string — it is a container, or dropping execution from the suite.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny file-write*)
(allow file-write* (subpath (param "WORKDIR")))
(allow file-write* (literal "/dev/null")
                   (literal "/dev/stdout")
                   (literal "/dev/stderr")
                   (literal "/dev/dtracehelper"))
